  "note": "Shows the whole Amateur Extra allocation: General, Technician and Novice privileges are narrower. On 60 m the four channels allow 100 W ERP and 5351.5-5366.5 kHz allows 9.15 W ERP. Voice is not allowed below 3600, 7125, 14150, 18110, 21200, 24930 or 28300 kHz. See 47 CFR 97.301 and 97.305. On VHF and UHF: 144.0-144.1 MHz is CW only (97.305). 420-430 MHz: no transmitting north of Line A, and FCC land mobile stations are protected within 80.5 km of Buffalo, Cleveland and Detroit (97.303(m)); the IC-9700 tunes 430-450 only. On 430-450 MHz and 23 cm amateurs must not cause harmful interference to, and must accept interference from, the services listed in 97.303(d), (m) and (o); ARRL adds that avoiding FAA radars may limit 23 cm use nearby. ARRL names no digital-voice calling frequency on these bands. FT8, FT4, MSK144, Q65 and WSPR markers are WSJT-X defaults, not ARRL plan frequencies. The non-amateur bands are only offered on a receiver, and a guide to what's where, not a statement of who may use it.",
